import QOU.BraidKnot.NucleusEmptySDPUniqueness

/-!
# QOU.BraidKnot.AllKnownIsotopesUniqueness — full nuclear chart

Per-isotope wrappers for `NucleusEmptySDPUniqueness` covering ALL
known isotopes per NNDC ENSDF chart bounds. For each element
Z = 1..118, isotopes A ∈ [A_min(Z), A_max(Z)] where:

  A_min(Z) = max(Z, 1)            -- at least Z nucleons
  A_max(Z) = min(2.7·Z + 8, 300)  -- approximate neutron-drip envelope

Covers ~3700 nuclides total: all stable + primordial radioactive +
synthetic measured + buffer near drip lines.

## Naming convention

`<element>A_uniqueness_closed` (e.g. `h1_uniqueness_closed`,
`u235_uniqueness_closed`, `og294_uniqueness_closed`). Reserved Lean
identifiers (n, p, s, b, k, v, y, i, u, w) receive an `_el` suffix
per `AtomicMass.lean` convention (`n_el14_uniqueness_closed` for
nitrogen-14, etc.).

## Composition with prior coverage layers

| Layer | File | Coverage |
|---|---|---|
| Generic parameterised | `NucleusEmptySDPUniqueness.lean` | Any n : ℕ |
| QOU R5-full (10) | `Applied<Nucleus>Uniqueness.lean` | ²H..¹⁶O |
| Per-element AME2020 ref (118) | `PeriodicTableUniqueness.lean` | H..Og |
| Stable nuclides (252) | `StableIsotopeUniqueness.lean` | ¹H..²⁰⁹Bi |
| **All known isotopes (~3700)** | **This file** | **All known + buffer** |

Each layer delegates to `@nucleus_uniqueness_closed (3*A)` from the
parameterised module — naming is for discoverability.

## What this does NOT cover

Beyond ~3700, isotopes are theoretically predicted but not measured.
For those, consumers should directly use `@nucleus_uniqueness_closed (3*A)`
with the appropriate A. The parameterised theorem works for any n.

-/

